Max-Heap Sort
Definition
Max-Heap Sort is a comparison-based sorting algorithm that uses a Max Heap data structure to sort elements in ascending order.
A Max Heap is a complete binary tree in which the value of every parent node is greater than or equal to the values of its children.
In Heap Sort, the given array is first converted into a Max Heap. The largest element is then placed at the end of the array,
and the remaining elements are heapified. This process is repeated until the complete array is sorted.
Time Complexity
Best Case: O(n log n)
Average Case: O(n log n)
Worst Case: O(n log n)
Auxiliary Space
Max-Heap Sort requires O(1) auxiliary space when implemented using an iterative heapify operation
because the sorting is performed in-place within the original array.
No additional array is required to store the sorted elements.
If heapify is implemented recursively, the recursion stack can require O(log n) space. 
However, an iterative implementation maintains O(1) auxiliary space.

Conclusion
Max-Heap Sort is an efficient comparison-based sorting algorithm that uses a Max Heap to arrange elements in ascending order.
It provides a guaranteed O(n log n) time complexity in the best, average, and worst cases. Its in-place implementation requires only O(1) auxiliary space, 
making it useful when predictable performance and low additional memory usage are required.

